WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:07.640
- Our next speaker is Edward Cooke,

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:10.370
Charles F Montgomery Professor
of the History of Art,

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:12.830
and Director of the Center of Study

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:15.230
in American Decorative
Arts and Material Culture.

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:17.800
As well as Professor of American Studies.

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:20.790
Professor Cooke focuses upon
American Material Culture

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:22.110
and Decorative Arts.

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:23.217
His books include

00:00:23.217 --> 00:00:25.477
"Making Furniture in
Preindustrial America,"

00:00:25.477 --> 00:00:29.790
"The Social Economy of Newtown
and Woodbury, Connecticut"

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:32.980
and "Inventing Boston Design,
Production and Consumption"

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:36.143
in the Atlantic world 1680 to 1720,

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:38.730
both of which focus upon the context

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:42.060
of craftsman client relations
in colonial North America.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:46.340
He's also written extensively
on modern craft, historicizing

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:49.000
and extricating more
recent forms of production

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.820
and has been co-curator
and publication author

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:54.020
of six different exhibitions.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:54.857
He is currently writing

00:00:54.857 --> 00:00:59.857
"Global objects towards the
Connected sorry, Arts History."

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:02.610
Cooke's work in expanding
the field of decorative arts

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:03.780
has led to several awards

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:05.450
including the Iris Foundation Award

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:07.910
for Outstanding Contribution
to the Decorative Arts

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:10.500
the Furniture Society's
Award of Distinction,

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.670
election as an honorary fellow
of the American Craft Council

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:15.060
and College Art Association,

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:17.450
Distinguished Teacher
of Art History Award.

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:19.000
Please welcome professor Cooke.

00:01:20.057 --> 00:01:23.057
(audience clapping)

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:25.787
- Thanks so much Matthew,

00:01:25.787 --> 00:01:28.940
it's really nice to be
participating in this idea

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:32.770
of interdisciplinary
humanities to reassert

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:37.500
the fact that humanities
is not a dead kind of topic

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:42.240
and in an age of big data and
in different kinds of stem,

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:46.100
but actually thinking
can we look at humanities

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:48.240
in a expanded field in some ways,

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:53.240
and when I have started to
get into sort of dealing

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:56.560
with objects within an
art history department,

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:00.140
which used to be considered the minor arts

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:01.753
in the old slide library,

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:07.660
how do you make them
part of the real center

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:11.250
of a discipline like art history?

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:14.050
And one of the moves that I
felt like was really essential

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:18.310
is traditional art history
has been very ocular central.

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:20.280
That is it's all about the eye,

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:24.660
the eye and the mind and
it's visual analysis.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:27.680
And sometimes you might describe it

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:29.870
as being object centered.

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:33.090
Some would even say object
or maybe art obsessed,

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:34.610
work of art obsessed.

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:36.210
And I wanted to flip that a little bit

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:40.240
and talk about object
driven kinds of inquiry.

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:42.480
In other words think about
objects from the inside out

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:44.850
rather than the outside in,

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:46.830
and sometimes not even
thinking about the end

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:49.250
of just sort of staying on the surface.

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:53.620
And so part of what I've
been about particularly

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:57.010
within last 10 years is developing a sense

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:58.800
of what I call material literacy

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:02.090
or maybe material intelligence,

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:04.490
that is knowledge of
materials and techniques

00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:08.120
and how those can lead to
new and innovative questions.

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:10.440
And I think of this not as a substitute

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:14.270
for digital humanities but
actually it's complimentary

00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:18.870
in terms of how we might open
up the idea of humanities

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:21.320
into a broader sensibility.

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:23.150
So one of the things that's really key

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:26.960
is to again, sort of
thinking about the stability

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:31.960
of materials and techniques
versus the permeability.

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:37.410
There's so many possibilities
of materials techniques

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:39.330
and what really leads to this idea

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:40.610
that I've developed in this new book

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:42.990
about interconnected art history.

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:46.070
It's not a linear path from A to B

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:48.690
but actually it's got
multiple layers directions

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:50.980
that are layered up on top of one another

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:54.370
to create this kind of rich mix.

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:58.340
So how do you go about teaching

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:01.260
from the inside out in some respects,

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:05.935
and part of this and Sue
Walker knows this well

00:04:05.935 --> 00:04:10.935
in terms of my interest in
asking questions of materials,

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:15.050
that is, I was always struck by people

00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:16.420
when I was doing my graduate work

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:21.420
who would just simply stay
true to ideas of style terms.

00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:25.270
And to me, I always had questions,

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:28.580
why did somebody over theorize
the curving of a panel

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:30.540
in the denim Medfield Chess,

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:32.790
and talked about the
use of these compasses

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:34.310
to lay out all these different lines.

00:04:34.310 --> 00:04:36.670
And I decided to do a carving exercise,

00:04:36.670 --> 00:04:37.910
having never done something like this

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:41.290
and realized a compass is
used in four different areas

00:04:41.290 --> 00:04:44.310
but that's it, the rest of
it is guided by the the curve

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:45.700
of the carving couch.

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:47.880
If you had the right four tools,

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:51.800
I could carve that in 20
minutes, light bulb goes off,

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:53.660
they over theorize it
without understanding

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:55.240
sort of from the inside out.

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:57.670
And so I did a number of projects

00:04:57.670 --> 00:04:59.860
while I was writing my dissertation,

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:02.540
that I was asking
questions that had come up

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:07.220
in what I was studying,
what I was writing about.

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:09.960
So a great way to actually
write a dissertation,

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:12.023
making things at the same time.

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:17.050
It then continued on
as I tried to acquire,

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:19.441
not that I was gonna
become a full-time maker.

00:05:19.441 --> 00:05:22.380
I had no illusions of this whatsoever,

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:26.450
but could I expose myself
to trying different things.

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:29.880
So doing a turning residency
down in Pennsylvania

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:34.610
for a week working with these
international turning stars

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:37.630
from France, from Australia,
from the United States,

00:05:37.630 --> 00:05:40.160
learning some basics, be able
to make a series of objects

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:42.200
with each of them, so to
spending a day with each

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:45.580
of these international residents

00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:49.010
and sort of working within
their vein, if you will.

00:05:49.010 --> 00:05:52.650
And realizing contrary to
what most people thought,

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:55.810
a lay this not a industrial tool

00:05:55.810 --> 00:05:57.370
that predetermines the result,

00:05:57.370 --> 00:06:01.503
it's merely a tool and you
can use it in multiple ways.

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:04.860
I filed that trying glassblowing,

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:05.940
trying block printing,

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:10.100
everywhere I go and visit
makers throughout the world.

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:12.290
I'll try my hand at something.

00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:13.990
I'll try hammering copper in Pune

00:06:14.853 --> 00:06:16.970
and what you end up in here,

00:06:16.970 --> 00:06:19.340
sort of like learning
to work on the ground

00:06:19.340 --> 00:06:23.090
and learning to use your feet
using other parts of your body

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:25.830
as a way of biomechanic feedback

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:30.830
and how that affects the
work posture and counters.

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:36.070
It's kind of a Western
perspective on serious work,

00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:40.950
you stand at a bench and you
sit upright the whole time

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:43.580
and you sort of over
jig some of your tools,

00:06:43.580 --> 00:06:45.880
here working on the ground,

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:47.470
there are many different advantages

00:06:47.470 --> 00:06:49.180
for certain types of work.

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:51.969
So it's this kind of
exploration that was so key

00:06:51.969 --> 00:06:55.063
that I then wanted to
take into the classroom.

00:06:56.350 --> 00:06:57.380
And one of the things

00:06:57.380 --> 00:07:01.510
that's really such a
strength of teaching any kind

00:07:01.510 --> 00:07:03.830
of art history at Yale
obviously is collections.

00:07:03.830 --> 00:07:08.410
And it's not just the museums
themselves and what's on view,

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:10.120
but it's also what's in storage,

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:13.430
it's the conservation department,

00:07:13.430 --> 00:07:16.560
the scientific research
department or IPCH,

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:18.920
these are all ways of thinking

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:21.320
about objects from the inside out,

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:24.410
and so when I teach classes,

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:26.300
what I'm also trying to do is say,

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:30.560
yes, visual analysis is
important but so it's touch.

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:33.110
So it's feeling the heft of
something, feeling surface,

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:36.090
being able to turn something
upside down, look inside that.

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:39.270
And so a lot of my courses
with the help of colleagues

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:41.050
at the art gallery have been,

00:07:41.050 --> 00:07:43.670
I don't know any other university museum

00:07:44.925 --> 00:07:47.820
that dedicate so much
space to study rooms,

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:50.360
and which you can bring
objects off of you,

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:51.380
and you can handle things.

00:07:51.380 --> 00:07:55.621
You can pass them around many different.

00:07:55.621 --> 00:07:58.860
I've been to a different gatherings.

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:00.950
And the Mellon Foundation has funded

00:08:00.950 --> 00:08:02.610
in terms of trying to get museum people

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:03.840
and academics together.

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:06.540
And there's some people
who feel so excited

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:09.310
that they pass objects around in trays,

00:08:09.310 --> 00:08:11.150
no touching but just
passing around in tray

00:08:11.150 --> 00:08:13.053
and think that's education.

00:08:14.170 --> 00:08:16.880
But it is, it's fear of
objects in some respects.

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:20.640
And what's wonderful is the
way in which colleagues here

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:23.780
work with you to establish
the right parameters,

00:08:23.780 --> 00:08:25.730
to make this a safe endeavor,

00:08:25.730 --> 00:08:27.910
to make sure that students are trained,

00:08:27.910 --> 00:08:30.490
teaching fellows are trained
in how to handle objects,

00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:32.940
how to model good behavior with objects

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:35.180
so that the students actually become

00:08:35.180 --> 00:08:39.340
the most respectful kinds
of users of the collection.

00:08:39.340 --> 00:08:41.210
They understand responsibility.

00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:43.510
If you tell them about
it, you give it to them

00:08:43.510 --> 00:08:45.640
you model it for them.

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:49.180
So this is just sort of,
what I do is I teach,

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:51.320
teaching fellows how to teach with objects

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:53.060
rather than, front of them

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:56.843
which I think is another really
important kind of maneuver.

00:08:58.010 --> 00:09:02.870
And then they go ahead
and teach their students.

00:09:02.870 --> 00:09:04.750
So what you're doing
is you're having effect

00:09:04.750 --> 00:09:08.840
on two different generations of scholars,

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:09.920
but the graduate students,

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:11.220
we only had to teach with objects

00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:14.800
as well as the undergraduates
exposure to kinds of questions

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:17.460
they could ask of objects

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:20.520
in terms of this kind
of global perspective.

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:24.990
And so here is it, you can
tell it's mask time from 2021.

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:28.210
And we are back in the classroom teaching.

00:09:28.210 --> 00:09:31.170
This was the hardest
thing of course to teach,

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:34.210
object driven inquiry remotely.

00:09:34.210 --> 00:09:35.100
We tried to get around it,

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:37.920
but there's nothing like the
satisfaction in that students,

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:39.700
you could tell not only
were they happy to be back

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:40.670
in the classroom together

00:09:40.670 --> 00:09:45.143
but they also liked being
around objects yet again.

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:52.050
There are wonderful resources
not simply in central campus,

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:55.440
but west campus becomes a wonderful area

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:59.090
where you've got collection study centers,

00:09:59.090 --> 00:10:01.960
the whole furniture study which
used to be on York Street,

00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:04.790
it's out there and it
becomes this incredible lab

00:10:04.790 --> 00:10:07.300
that's basically OpenStack storage

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:10.530
if you want to use a library
term that you can browse

00:10:10.530 --> 00:10:13.360
that I actually run a first-year seminar

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:16.020
to get somebody early on in their career

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:17.710
and get people who are engineers,

00:10:17.710 --> 00:10:21.653
who are art majors who
might be history majors,

00:10:22.550 --> 00:10:26.200
quite a swath of people
who might take this course.

00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:29.620
And we meet, that's the
classroom, the furniture study,

00:10:29.620 --> 00:10:33.270
we go out in a shuttle every
week, one o'clock shuttle,

00:10:33.270 --> 00:10:34.960
we're out there for two hours.

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:37.240
I can run class on the shuttle,

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:39.220
Roxanna was nice to
get a dedicated shuttle

00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:41.320
so I could actually have Q and A

00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:45.240
and some other kind of
housekeeping on the way out.

00:10:45.240 --> 00:10:48.690
And then also be able to
teach graduate seminars

00:10:48.690 --> 00:10:51.850
that would oftentimes
involve handling, looking,

00:10:51.850 --> 00:10:55.290
talking about objects
with them right with us,

00:10:55.290 --> 00:10:56.763
not just looking at images.

00:10:58.330 --> 00:11:01.060
The Wurtele Study Center
where a lot of the collections

00:11:01.060 --> 00:11:06.060
of non furniture materials
from American Decorative Arts

00:11:06.980 --> 00:11:10.409
ceramics, glass, textiles et cetera.

00:11:10.409 --> 00:11:12.560
It's an amazing, again open storage

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:15.270
but unlike the Luce center at the Met,

00:11:15.270 --> 00:11:19.100
which is just encased
in captivity in Plexi,

00:11:19.100 --> 00:11:22.500
this is all you can call
things and bring them out

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:25.650
for a particular class,
so with Denise Leidy,

00:11:25.650 --> 00:11:29.310
who's the curator of Asian
art we ran a joint class

00:11:29.310 --> 00:11:33.780
on Lacquer in a global context
not just East Asian lacquer,

00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:37.240
but also Japanning from Europe and America

00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:42.240
to talk about non-bond
export ware from Japan,

00:11:43.570 --> 00:11:45.556
but handling things, looking at things,

00:11:45.556 --> 00:11:49.960
asking questions at the objects
as we looked inside of them

00:11:49.960 --> 00:11:51.853
was a critical part of it.

00:11:54.530 --> 00:11:57.780
I've also tried to instead
of follow the principles

00:11:57.780 --> 00:11:59.863
of John Dewey, learning by doing,

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:03.130
and what I try to do is is sort of,

00:12:03.130 --> 00:12:07.610
put making exercises into
various different courses.

00:12:07.610 --> 00:12:09.830
Initially in the old furniture study,

00:12:09.830 --> 00:12:11.410
they had sort of a shop

00:12:11.410 --> 00:12:14.910
and I would bring in a friend
of mine, Peter Follansbee

00:12:14.910 --> 00:12:17.630
who used to be the joiner
at Plymouth Plantation

00:12:17.630 --> 00:12:19.970
an open air museum up in Massachusetts.

00:12:19.970 --> 00:12:23.180
And we worked out to the
different exercises we could do

00:12:23.180 --> 00:12:25.080
within two hours to give students a chance

00:12:25.080 --> 00:12:27.570
to split out greenwood, to play in it,

00:12:27.570 --> 00:12:29.810
to learn how to do some
preliminary carving,

00:12:29.810 --> 00:12:31.980
not to make something in its entirety,

00:12:31.980 --> 00:12:33.613
but just get a few principles.

00:12:35.110 --> 00:12:36.150
This is something that I've done,

00:12:36.150 --> 00:12:39.390
carried forward as another
part of west campus.

00:12:39.390 --> 00:12:41.390
Scott Strobel, when he
was head of west campus,

00:12:41.390 --> 00:12:45.970
gave me a space out there to
use as a low tech maker space

00:12:45.970 --> 00:12:49.850
that I could store some tools,
some materials out there,

00:12:49.850 --> 00:12:52.200
and I've started to incorporate into this.

00:12:52.200 --> 00:12:54.850
So that's where I've
done a woodworking now

00:12:54.850 --> 00:12:58.770
on the left for a
first-year seminar in 2019,

00:12:58.770 --> 00:13:03.327
I had another one class on
vessels in which one class,

00:13:05.030 --> 00:13:07.900
we ended up hammering
copper disks into bowls

00:13:07.900 --> 00:13:10.940
to learn something about hammering,

00:13:10.940 --> 00:13:12.943
the malleability of metal.

00:13:14.580 --> 00:13:17.460
This is again sort of greenwood-working

00:13:17.460 --> 00:13:19.550
I did for this material literacy

00:13:19.550 --> 00:13:23.080
and then embodied artisinal
knowledge using just basic tools

00:13:23.080 --> 00:13:27.420
like clubs, froes, hatchets, and knives,

00:13:27.420 --> 00:13:29.760
and sending people to work,

00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:32.120
the advantage of fall in New England.

00:13:32.120 --> 00:13:33.960
You can work outside.

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:38.200
So one year we were carving
spoons on west campus,

00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:40.200
around the fire pit.

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:42.390
It wasn't available this year.

00:13:42.390 --> 00:13:45.713
So we went to one of the
courtyards of west campus

00:13:45.713 --> 00:13:50.190
in that collection study
center with a shaving horse.

00:13:50.190 --> 00:13:53.169
Again, hatchets, froes,
splitting out wood,

00:13:53.169 --> 00:13:56.360
green, moist wood and then working

00:13:56.360 --> 00:13:59.493
on different spatulas and the like,

00:14:00.560 --> 00:14:02.810
all this is working closely

00:14:02.810 --> 00:14:06.230
with different kinds of artisans.

00:14:06.230 --> 00:14:10.810
So Peter Follansbee, Andre
Garces was helpful with the wood,

00:14:10.810 --> 00:14:13.860
Joan Parcher, a metal
smith up in Providence.

00:14:13.860 --> 00:14:17.470
I've worked with her to developing
a very basic kit of tools

00:14:17.470 --> 00:14:22.470
that I could give students to
work with making copper disks

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:24.820
learning how to shape something

00:14:24.820 --> 00:14:29.513
with a ball-peen hammer on a steel block.

00:14:31.910 --> 00:14:36.110
I worked with Andrew Hamilton
developing another session

00:14:36.110 --> 00:14:38.240
on spinning and fiber.

00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:40.480
And watching students get frustrated,

00:14:40.480 --> 00:14:42.003
trying to use a drop spindle,

00:14:43.340 --> 00:14:46.030
these very basic sorts of endeavors,

00:14:46.030 --> 00:14:48.090
but you learn so much about the materials

00:14:48.090 --> 00:14:52.400
and the feedback on them that
you can just see the ways

00:14:52.400 --> 00:14:55.100
in which when you have
them students in class,

00:14:55.100 --> 00:14:58.620
the way they get absorbed,
time flies for them.

00:14:58.620 --> 00:15:02.200
I've also sent out kits,
ceramic kits, metal kits,

00:15:02.200 --> 00:15:05.440
and weaving kits to students
for the undergraduate class

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:08.020
that they do in their own dorms.

00:15:08.020 --> 00:15:10.340
This was a COVID related sort of endeavor.

00:15:10.340 --> 00:15:14.470
And again, students just raved
about learning off screen

00:15:14.470 --> 00:15:16.780
and being able to sort of learn

00:15:16.780 --> 00:15:19.320
so much and then apply
what they've learned

00:15:19.320 --> 00:15:22.883
in the grappling with the
material to their own work.

00:15:23.740 --> 00:15:26.250
Again, with Andrew, we did some weaving.

00:15:26.250 --> 00:15:28.950
This is some of the cardboard loom weaving

00:15:28.950 --> 00:15:30.220
we did for the undergraduate,

00:15:30.220 --> 00:15:34.070
just providing them with a cotton warp,

00:15:34.070 --> 00:15:36.730
thread warp, cardboard
that sets up at the loom

00:15:36.730 --> 00:15:39.360
and then a series of acrylics
that are sort of wool like,

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:42.260
so this is two different students' work

00:15:42.260 --> 00:15:43.510
just completed this year.

00:15:45.520 --> 00:15:49.150
I've worked with Mark Potter
to work on sort of different

00:15:49.150 --> 00:15:51.680
sets of recipes for clay

00:15:52.810 --> 00:15:55.570
that you can add different elements to it.

00:15:55.570 --> 00:15:58.450
You can create different
sorts of appearances.

00:15:58.450 --> 00:16:00.970
We did this exercise in
which we gave students

00:16:00.970 --> 00:16:02.550
an hour in which they worked

00:16:02.550 --> 00:16:07.550
with prescribed proportion
recipes to make something.

00:16:07.940 --> 00:16:10.570
And then we gave them unlimited materials

00:16:10.570 --> 00:16:12.430
and had them measure out.

00:16:12.430 --> 00:16:14.040
These were the measuring devices they had,

00:16:14.040 --> 00:16:16.190
just a piece of wood
with holes drilled in it.

00:16:16.190 --> 00:16:18.910
So they had to work out
proportions in their minds

00:16:18.910 --> 00:16:20.450
of what these materials were gonna do.

00:16:20.450 --> 00:16:22.570
This is the kit of tools we used

00:16:22.570 --> 00:16:26.910
to then work with either
regular clay, mixed

00:16:26.910 --> 00:16:29.570
or we could start adding
different kinds of ingredients.

00:16:29.570 --> 00:16:31.740
So they made their own recipe.

00:16:31.740 --> 00:16:35.430
Once they made them, we
made them making test strips

00:16:35.430 --> 00:16:39.790
to see, here are these different formulas

00:16:39.790 --> 00:16:42.330
when they fired some of
them didn't do so well,

00:16:42.330 --> 00:16:44.830
they shrunk, they bent around.

00:16:44.830 --> 00:16:46.330
Some of them were not very stable

00:16:46.330 --> 00:16:48.690
and some of them were disasters.

00:16:48.690 --> 00:16:50.354
I feel like some of the
students didn't know

00:16:50.354 --> 00:16:52.260
when you're baking for instance,

00:16:52.260 --> 00:16:54.930
you do dry ingredients
first and then add liquid.

00:16:54.930 --> 00:16:57.500
There was some people who did liquid first

00:16:57.500 --> 00:16:59.180
and just peeled all the,

00:16:59.180 --> 00:17:00.570
we were working on sheet rock

00:17:00.570 --> 00:17:02.700
and it just like made a mess on the board.

00:17:02.700 --> 00:17:05.760
So they were working on, you
learn a lot along the way.

00:17:05.760 --> 00:17:07.160
And then these are just
some of the objects.

00:17:07.160 --> 00:17:09.780
I had everybody do a
test strip in an object

00:17:09.780 --> 00:17:13.440
with a given formula that they had to mix.

00:17:13.440 --> 00:17:15.573
And then also with their own invention,

00:17:18.280 --> 00:17:20.590
what I've done recently
for the undergraduates

00:17:20.590 --> 00:17:22.900
is working with Penelope Van Grinsven,

00:17:22.900 --> 00:17:25.100
who's taken on responsibility

00:17:25.100 --> 00:17:28.170
of the five undergraduate pottery studios,

00:17:28.170 --> 00:17:31.130
is to develop a kit that you could coil.

00:17:31.130 --> 00:17:34.060
And this sort of has a
folding board with canvas,

00:17:34.060 --> 00:17:36.490
a cutting wire two and a half pounds,

00:17:36.490 --> 00:17:38.973
three pounds of clay,
a bucket with a sponge,

00:17:40.220 --> 00:17:42.190
sort of a needle tool, a rib,

00:17:42.190 --> 00:17:45.290
everything you need to
make objects like this,

00:17:45.290 --> 00:17:47.360
these are not thrown on a wheel.

00:17:47.360 --> 00:17:49.930
These are all coiled or slab construction.

00:17:49.930 --> 00:17:52.990
These are undergraduates this past fall,

00:17:52.990 --> 00:17:56.610
this semester who are
making these sorts of things

00:17:56.610 --> 00:17:57.870
with this kind of exercise,

00:17:57.870 --> 00:17:59.870
just given clay, a bending wheel

00:17:59.870 --> 00:18:01.800
to sort of like the Lazy Susan,

00:18:01.800 --> 00:18:04.833
just to get some sort of
sense of what's possible.

00:18:06.760 --> 00:18:10.650
So in teaching material literacy,

00:18:10.650 --> 00:18:14.530
Yale's got this incredible
opportunity in terms of access

00:18:14.530 --> 00:18:17.730
to collections, things
that will inspire you,

00:18:17.730 --> 00:18:22.000
ways to then try out
making yourself exposed

00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:24.130
to different sorts of materials.

00:18:24.130 --> 00:18:27.820
And then also taking that
knowledge back to the classroom,

00:18:27.820 --> 00:18:29.490
looking, handling at objects,

00:18:29.490 --> 00:18:32.570
not simply looking at
them under a vitrine.

00:18:32.570 --> 00:18:35.120
And it's just a couple
things that are important

00:18:35.120 --> 00:18:38.510
to this idea of the pedagogy of teaching

00:18:38.510 --> 00:18:41.600
object-driven inquiry is in addition

00:18:41.600 --> 00:18:44.570
to having objects study
classrooms in abundance.

00:18:44.570 --> 00:18:46.430
And it's wonderful to see the Peabody

00:18:46.430 --> 00:18:48.990
with their renovations
doing the same thing,

00:18:48.990 --> 00:18:51.390
but to also start to realize,

00:18:51.390 --> 00:18:54.043
well, if you can have a
messy space, can you design,

00:18:55.680 --> 00:19:00.020
can you sort of work with
sympathetic knowledgeable makers?

00:19:00.020 --> 00:19:02.270
Nobody, one person can do it all.

00:19:02.270 --> 00:19:06.070
So oftentimes it's a
collaboration like to and fro-ing

00:19:06.070 --> 00:19:08.440
with Joan Parcher, what
would be a good exercise?

00:19:08.440 --> 00:19:10.788
What I'd tried the first time didn't work.

00:19:10.788 --> 00:19:14.480
And that's when I got her
into the conversation.

00:19:14.480 --> 00:19:16.810
These are things that I
was never trained in it,

00:19:16.810 --> 00:19:20.470
but it's wonderful to sort of
develop these ideas together.

00:19:20.470 --> 00:19:22.050
And then I think the other
thing that's important

00:19:22.050 --> 00:19:23.490
is thinking about time

00:19:23.490 --> 00:19:27.680
in terms of what can you
accomplish within two hours?

00:19:27.680 --> 00:19:28.630
What's a project?

00:19:28.630 --> 00:19:32.010
And so many Yale students
are perfectionists

00:19:32.010 --> 00:19:33.480
who have an idea of what they wanna make.

00:19:33.480 --> 00:19:35.690
And you have to get
them to shift that aside

00:19:35.690 --> 00:19:37.440
and think, just go with a process,

00:19:37.440 --> 00:19:39.820
don't worry about what you wanna exert

00:19:39.820 --> 00:19:43.490
your will on something, what
can you learn along the way?

00:19:43.490 --> 00:19:48.170
So it's been a fabulous
kind of path to go down

00:19:48.170 --> 00:19:51.590
to thinking about the material
humanities in some respects,

00:19:51.590 --> 00:19:55.100
and to thinking about what
Yale really allows us to do.

00:19:55.100 --> 00:19:58.130
And I hope that you get
a chance to sort of visit

00:19:58.130 --> 00:20:00.450
with some of the other information tables

00:20:00.450 --> 00:20:05.450
from the, just thinking
of the Wurtele Center

00:20:05.720 --> 00:20:09.237
and the art gallery study centers
in west campus of the IPCH

00:20:10.140 --> 00:20:12.160
Institute for Preservation
of Cultural Heritage

00:20:12.160 --> 00:20:13.910
and the research scientists there,

00:20:13.910 --> 00:20:17.340
conservation staff for
the various museums.

00:20:17.340 --> 00:20:19.120
It's this kind of package that you've got

00:20:19.120 --> 00:20:21.280
so many people you can turn to,

00:20:21.280 --> 00:20:25.860
to ask questions and to work
together to sort of move

00:20:25.860 --> 00:20:28.040
some of this project forward.

00:20:28.040 --> 00:20:29.920
So I'm happy to answer questions.

00:20:29.920 --> 00:20:32.320
I just want to sort of tempt you

00:20:32.320 --> 00:20:33.753
in terms of what's possible.

00:20:35.022 --> 00:20:38.022
(audience clapping)

00:20:45.290 --> 00:20:46.500
- [Student] I'm happy to start off.

00:20:46.500 --> 00:20:49.300
So when we were talking
about the digital humanities,

00:20:49.300 --> 00:20:51.040
the question of sort of the skills,

00:20:51.040 --> 00:20:53.500
the coding skills and
whatnot that are needed

00:20:53.500 --> 00:20:56.140
are not sort of part of the
curriculum that we understand.

00:20:56.140 --> 00:20:58.900
Are there ways in which you
can see this sort of work

00:20:58.900 --> 00:21:02.130
being incorporated into the
curriculum or how you've.

00:21:02.130 --> 00:21:03.810
- Yeah, I mean, what I've managed to do,

00:21:03.810 --> 00:21:07.990
the question is how to integrate
this into the curriculum.

00:21:07.990 --> 00:21:09.330
And that's why I wanted to actually show

00:21:09.330 --> 00:21:11.920
what I've been doing
in some of the classes.

00:21:11.920 --> 00:21:15.850
So with the undergraduates
I haven't necessarily

00:21:15.850 --> 00:21:17.110
other than the furniture class,

00:21:17.110 --> 00:21:20.620
I haven't taught them
in class time itself,

00:21:20.620 --> 00:21:22.640
but I developed these kits last year

00:21:22.640 --> 00:21:24.760
when people were remote,

00:21:24.760 --> 00:21:27.380
that actually had them
pick up or mail to them,

00:21:27.380 --> 00:21:31.650
if they were truly off campus
and they would make things,

00:21:31.650 --> 00:21:33.600
photograph them and then ship the kit.

00:21:33.600 --> 00:21:35.630
Well, the weaving kit was inexpensive.

00:21:35.630 --> 00:21:38.990
So that was just a throw
away but the the ceramic kit

00:21:38.990 --> 00:21:41.880
and the metal kit ended up investing

00:21:41.880 --> 00:21:45.530
25 to $35 for each of those kits.

00:21:45.530 --> 00:21:49.780
So that's a way in which
I felt like that became

00:21:49.780 --> 00:21:52.480
an assignment, making and reflecting

00:21:52.480 --> 00:21:54.470
and really giving them parameters

00:21:54.470 --> 00:21:56.263
in which they could succeed.

00:21:57.930 --> 00:21:59.530
And they had to write
reflections, as I said,

00:21:59.530 --> 00:22:03.250
so it's not just simply winging it

00:22:03.250 --> 00:22:04.810
and then that's the end of it.

00:22:04.810 --> 00:22:07.360
But by reflecting and giving them,

00:22:07.360 --> 00:22:08.710
prompting them with certain questions

00:22:08.710 --> 00:22:13.160
was a way to dig deeper
into what this meant

00:22:13.160 --> 00:22:14.510
in terms of the course.

00:22:14.510 --> 00:22:17.930
For the graduate students
I do it within class

00:22:17.930 --> 00:22:20.680
because you've got a
longer period of time.

00:22:20.680 --> 00:22:25.620
And I will try to develop say
three or four making exercises

00:22:25.620 --> 00:22:27.360
over the semester.

00:22:27.360 --> 00:22:31.200
So this course I'm teaching
now on embodied knowledge,

00:22:31.200 --> 00:22:32.470
we did woodworking first

00:22:32.470 --> 00:22:35.328
and the emphasis there
was on the materials.

00:22:35.328 --> 00:22:36.870
What does the material, what's
the agency of the material?

00:22:36.870 --> 00:22:37.895
Cause it's greenwood

00:22:37.895 --> 00:22:41.390
and it's gonna go the way it wants to go.

00:22:41.390 --> 00:22:44.475
So you have to work with it
and then when we did metal,

00:22:44.475 --> 00:22:47.490
it was about thinking about the body

00:22:47.490 --> 00:22:49.860
'cause I had them working
at a bench on the ground,

00:22:49.860 --> 00:22:52.770
sitting all sorts of, just kept
moving their body positions

00:22:52.770 --> 00:22:55.840
around to get them finding
what's the right way.

00:22:55.840 --> 00:22:57.820
And again, they had to write reflections

00:22:57.820 --> 00:23:01.990
and then we just did pottery on Wednesday.

00:23:01.990 --> 00:23:04.330
And there I was trying
to get them to focus

00:23:04.330 --> 00:23:06.970
on the hand and again,
having them on the ground

00:23:06.970 --> 00:23:10.330
at a wheel coiling all sorts
of different movements.

00:23:10.330 --> 00:23:12.890
So there's a way to do
it, to incorporate it.

00:23:12.890 --> 00:23:14.850
And then there's also
like the undergraduates

00:23:14.850 --> 00:23:18.170
have this opportunity in
the residential colleges,

00:23:18.170 --> 00:23:20.020
that there are a number
of different studios,

00:23:20.020 --> 00:23:21.340
like five pottery studios.

00:23:21.340 --> 00:23:24.363
There's a weaving studio
in Morrison styles,

00:23:25.360 --> 00:23:27.540
but it's harder for the graduate students

00:23:27.540 --> 00:23:28.853
to gain access to that.

00:23:34.450 --> 00:23:37.890
But it's not, I'm not running
an art school course either.

00:23:37.890 --> 00:23:40.840
So it's that fine line,

00:23:40.840 --> 00:23:43.920
It's got a tie into
the theme, the reading,

00:23:43.920 --> 00:23:47.453
the intellectual content
of the course, yeah there.

00:23:52.545 --> 00:23:54.303
- [Student] I have two questions one is,

00:23:54.303 --> 00:23:59.303
I was wondering if you (indistinct)

00:24:01.070 --> 00:24:06.070
but I was also wondering if you
still teach this to faculty.

00:24:10.355 --> 00:24:14.390
- I have, there've been a couple of times

00:24:14.390 --> 00:24:19.390
that I had the opportunity to
have a teaching on west campus

00:24:23.396 --> 00:24:25.840
that was designed for
graduate students faculty

00:24:25.840 --> 00:24:27.050
and museum people.

00:24:27.050 --> 00:24:30.610
And it had different themes
and brought different people

00:24:30.610 --> 00:24:34.760
in to run different sorts of projects.

00:24:34.760 --> 00:24:38.707
So one was on this idea of spoons.

00:24:41.213 --> 00:24:45.690
I mean, we sort of have a
session which we had makers,

00:24:45.690 --> 00:24:49.530
artists talk about their
practice sort of one day,

00:24:49.530 --> 00:24:51.867
a second day would be making
things on west campus.

00:24:51.867 --> 00:24:56.867
And the third day would be
about exhibition practice.

00:24:58.820 --> 00:25:02.850
So there was one year we did
one that had sort of the idea

00:25:02.850 --> 00:25:05.620
of reproducibility that we had spoons.

00:25:05.620 --> 00:25:08.210
We cast spoons, we curve
spoons, we made clay spoons,

00:25:08.210 --> 00:25:11.890
we hammered copper spoons
and just kept rotating people

00:25:11.890 --> 00:25:14.370
around, always working
with the same spoon.

00:25:14.370 --> 00:25:17.010
And yet also had Andres Garces

00:25:17.010 --> 00:25:20.510
who was working in
conservation at that point,

00:25:20.510 --> 00:25:22.780
he had scanned one of these spoons.

00:25:22.780 --> 00:25:27.780
And then we did 3D
printing as well as CNC,

00:25:27.811 --> 00:25:29.740
subtractive technology in wood,

00:25:29.740 --> 00:25:33.040
and started working off
of what's the difference

00:25:33.040 --> 00:25:38.040
between a cast pewter spoon
and a CNC cut out spoon

00:25:38.770 --> 00:25:41.760
or a 3D printed spoon.

00:25:41.760 --> 00:25:45.000
And it was great conversations
just to sort of explode that,

00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:48.160
there was another year
we talked about the idea

00:25:48.160 --> 00:25:53.160
of repair and different kinds
of recalibration that we had.

00:26:01.650 --> 00:26:03.760
Christina Kim who's a textile artist,

00:26:03.760 --> 00:26:06.790
come in and talk about in a sewing circle.

00:26:06.790 --> 00:26:11.430
We had Sam Thomas hard-sunny
beater who came in

00:26:11.430 --> 00:26:16.100
and we had a beating exercise
to talk about reconciliation

00:26:16.100 --> 00:26:20.420
that he's been doing with the
Canadian boarding schools,

00:26:20.420 --> 00:26:23.460
indigenous people and
it was just incredible

00:26:23.460 --> 00:26:25.800
to think about reconciliation, repair

00:26:25.800 --> 00:26:30.100
and how making and materials
really lend themselves

00:26:30.100 --> 00:26:32.640
to this easygoing conversation.

00:26:32.640 --> 00:26:37.453
So that's been one thing it's
hard to do it every year.

00:26:38.370 --> 00:26:39.940
Sort of just coming up with these ideas,

00:26:39.940 --> 00:26:41.440
trying to mobilize people,

00:26:41.440 --> 00:26:45.710
I think in terms of
stories, anecdotes of it's.

00:26:49.520 --> 00:26:51.530
I mean what I've really been struck by

00:26:51.530 --> 00:26:54.120
with a graduate seminar
than when we're working

00:26:54.120 --> 00:26:55.840
with different materials,

00:26:55.840 --> 00:26:58.210
watching people all of a sudden just relax

00:26:58.210 --> 00:27:03.210
and start to realize I don't
have to be a perfectionist.

00:27:03.210 --> 00:27:05.960
I'm just, I'm going with
the flow, the idea flow.

00:27:05.960 --> 00:27:09.370
And one of the topics
that's come up this year

00:27:09.370 --> 00:27:11.680
is what does focus mean,
what is concentration?

00:27:11.680 --> 00:27:16.680
What does absorption and how
can you be focused in an area

00:27:16.930 --> 00:27:19.940
where you're all hammering
and all of a sudden like that,

00:27:19.940 --> 00:27:21.990
that white noise starts to become

00:27:21.990 --> 00:27:24.360
a way that you create
your own little bubble

00:27:24.360 --> 00:27:27.690
versus being outdoors
on a beautiful fall day.

00:27:27.690 --> 00:27:30.510
And you're just using a
simple little slide knife

00:27:30.510 --> 00:27:33.320
doing your final cuts on a spatula

00:27:33.320 --> 00:27:38.320
and you're just, you're
completely removed from the world.

00:27:38.320 --> 00:27:39.640
So it's interesting

00:27:39.640 --> 00:27:42.950
to just sort of watching
people's bodies relax,

00:27:42.950 --> 00:27:45.640
watching when they're
social with one another,

00:27:45.640 --> 00:27:48.100
when they're sort of
looking at their own work

00:27:48.100 --> 00:27:51.340
and you start to see
how it affects the way

00:27:51.340 --> 00:27:53.530
in which they're talking about objects

00:27:53.530 --> 00:27:54.753
in subsequent classes.

00:27:54.753 --> 00:27:57.173
I don't know if that answers
some of your question.

00:28:00.660 --> 00:28:01.493
Thanks Cindy.

00:28:03.334 --> 00:28:06.334
(audience clapping)

00:28:08.124 --> 00:28:10.707
(upbeat music)

